sec.p:
Since the switched inductor is controlled by an NMOS switch ($S_{Ls}$), it should be noted that due to the non-ideal characteristics of the switch, it can be equivalent to an on
-resistance ($r_{\text {on} }$) when turned on, and as two parasitic capacitances ($C_{\text {off} }$) to ground when turned off, as shown in Fig. 7. The presence of $r_{\text {on} }$ and $C_{\text {off} }$ will affect the equivalent inductance in each operation mode, which can be analyzed using a simplified lumped model of the switched-inductor-based quad-mode transformer, as shown in Fig. 8.
1)
When the switch $S_{Ls}$ is turned on corresponding to mode-2, the switched inductor is connected in series with the on
-resistance $r_{\text {on} }$, as shown in Fig. 8(a). The electrical parameters–namely $k_{\text {adj}}$, $k_{\text {opp}}$ and $L$–will be affected by $r_{\text {on} }$, resulting in modified values denoted as $k_{\text {adj},\text {on} }$, $k_{\text {opp},\text {on} }$ and $L_{\text {on}}$, respectively. The modified parameter $k_{\text {adj},\text {on} }$ can be derived based on Fig. 8(a) by analyzing $Z_{12}=V_{1}/I_{2}$ under the condition $I_{1}=I_{3}=I_{4}=0$, while $I_{\text {sw}}$ and $I_{2}$ provide current excitations. According to the mutual inductance voltage equation [25], the voltage $V_{1}$ can be written as follows: \begin{equation*} {V_{1}} = s{M_{\text {adj}}}{I_{2}} + s{M_{\text {sw}}}{I_{\text {sw}}} \tag {8}\end{equation*}where $M_{\text {sw}}=k_{\text {sw}}(LL_{\text {sw}})^{1/2}$ and $s={jw}$.
As the on
-resistance $r_{\text {on} }$ effectively connects both ends of the switched inductor, the Kirchhoff’s voltage law (KVL) equation can be derived as follows: \begin{equation*} 0 = s{L_{\text {sw}}}{I_{\text {sw}}} + s{M_{\text {sw}}} {I_{2}} + r_{\text {on} }{I_{\text {sw}}}. \tag {9}\end{equation*}
From (8) and (9), $Z_{12}$ can be derived as follows: \begin{equation*} Z_{12} = s{M_{\text {adj},\text {on} }} = s\left ({{{M_{\text {adj}}} - \frac {sM_{\text {sw}}^{2}}{s{L_{\text {sw}}} + {r_{\text {on} }}}}}\right ). \tag {10}\end{equation*}
It can be seen from (10) that the presence of $r_{\text {on} }$ leads to variation in the mutual inductance $M_{\text {adj}}$ between adjacent cores, where the modified value $M_{\text {adj},\text {on} }$ can be expressed as follows: \begin{equation*} {M_{\text {adj},\text {on} }} = {M_{\text {adj}}} - \frac {sM_{\text {sw}}^{2}}{s{L_{\text {sw}}} + {r_{\text {on} }}}. \tag {11}\end{equation*}
Similarly, $M_{\text {opp},\text {on} }$ and $L_{\text {on} }$ can be derived as follows: \begin{align*} {M_{\text {opp},\text {on} }} = {M_{\text {opp}}} - \frac {sM_{\text {sw}}^{2}}{s{L_{\text {sw}}} + {r_{\text {on} }}} \tag {12}\\ {L_{\text {on} }} = L - \frac {sM_{\text {sw}}^{2}}{s{L_{\text {sw}}} + {r_{\text {on} }}}. \tag {13}\end{align*}
Consequently, the equivalent inductance in mode-2 can be derived as follows: \begin{equation*} {L_{\text {mode},2}} = L + 2{M_{\text {adj}}} + {M_{\text {opp}}} - \frac {4sM_{\text {sw}}^{2}}{s{L_{\text {sw}}} + {r_{\text {on} }}}. \tag {14}\end{equation*}
As long as the size of the switch $S_{Ls}$ is sufficiently large, its on
-resistance $r_{\text {on} }$ can be neglected and the difference in equivalent inductances between mode-1 and -2 becomes $4k_{\text {sw}}^{2}L$. Therefore, by adjusting the spacing between $L$ and $L_{\text {sw}}$, the coupling coefficient $k_{\text {sw}}$ can be controlled, enabling the separation of $L_{\text {mode},2}$ from $L_{\text {mode},1}$, and allowing mode-2 to fill the frequency gap between mode-1 and -3.
2)
When the switch $S_{Ls}$ is turned off corresponding to mode-1, -3, and -4, the switched inductor is connected in series with the parasitic capacitance $C_{\text {off} }$, as shown in Fig. 8(b). Similar to the previous analysis, the only difference lies in replacing $r_{\text {on} }$ with $2/({sC}_{\text {off} })$. Thus, the equivalent modified $M_{\text {adj},\text {off} }$, $M_{\text {opp},\text {off} }$ and $L_{\text {off} }$ can be expressed as follows: \begin{align*} {M_{\text {adj},\text {off} }} & = {M_{\text {adj}}} - \frac {{s^{2}}M_{\text {sw}}^{2}{C_{\text {off} }}}{{s^{2}}{L_{\text {sw}}}{C_{\text {off} }} + 2} \tag {15}\\ {M_{\text {opp},\text {off} }} & = {M_{\text {opp}}} - \frac {{s^{2}}M_{\text {sw}}^{2}{C_{\text {off} }}}{{s^{2}}{L_{\text {sw}}}{C_{\text {off} }} + 2} \tag {16}\\ {L_{\text {off} }} & = L - \frac {{s^{2}}M_{\text {sw}}^{2}{C_{\text {off} }}}{{s^{2}}{L_{\text {sw}}}{C_{\text {off} }} + 2}. \tag {17}\end{align*}
As a result, the equivalent inductances in mode-1, -3, and -4 can be expressed as follows: \begin{align*} {L_{\text {mode},1}} & = L + 2{M_{\text {adj}}} + {M_{\text {opp}}} - \frac {4{s^{2}}M_{\text {sw}}^{2}{C_{\text {off} }}}{{s^{2}}{L_{\text {sw}}}{C_{\text {off} }} + 2} \\ & \approx L + 2{M_{\text {adj}}} + {M_{\text {opp}}} \tag {18}\\ {L_{\text {mode},3}} & = L_{\text {off} } - {M_{\text {opp},\text {off} }} = L - {M_{\text {opp}}} \tag {19}\\ {L_{\text {mode},4}} & = L_{\text {off} } - 2{M_{\text {adj},\text {off} }} + {M_{\text {opp},\text {off} }} \\ & = L - 2{M_{\text {adj}}} + {M_{\text {opp}}}. \tag {20}\end{align*}
In (18), the last term is a minor factor and can be neglected. Therefore, the equivalent inductance in mode-1 remains approximately unchanged compared to (5). In mode-3 and -4, the equivalent inductances remain consistent with (6) and (7), respectively, as indicated in (19) and (20). In other words, the equivalent inductances in mode-1, -3, and -4 are rarely affected by the switched inductor. In mode-1, the result comes from an expression approximation, while in mode-3 and -4, it can be explained by the fact that the currents induced in $L_{\text {sw}}$ by the surrounding tank inductors cancel each other out, which means that $L_{\text {sw}}$ is transparent in mode-1, -3, and -4.
Fig. 7.
NMOS switch of the switched inductor and its equivalent circuits in on and off states.
Fig. 8.
Simplified lumped model of the proposed transformer with the switch $S_{Ls}$ in (a) on and (b) off states.

Fig. 11(a) shows the quad-mode transformer for mode-3 with the switched inductor off, where ${k_{\text {adj},\text {off} }} = {M_{\text {adj},\text {off} }}/{L_{\text {off} }}$ and ${k_{\text {opp},\text {off} }} = {M_{\text {opp},\text {off} }}/{L_{\text {off} }}$. From the perspective of core-1, we split the inductor $L_{\text {off} }$ at its geometric center into two equal halves and retain only the couplings relevant to core-1, as shown in Fig. 11(b). To preserve the total mutual coupling for core-1, the coefficients satisfy $k_{1}{\,}+{\,}k_{2}{\,}={\,}\sqrt {2}k_{\text {adj},\text {off} }$ and $k_{3}{\,}+{\,}k_{4}{\,}={\,}\sqrt {2}k_{\text {opp},\text {off} }$. Further inspection of the transformer layout in Fig. 9(a) shows that $k_{1}\lt k_{2}$ and $k_{3}=k_{4}$. Under these conditions, the equivalent inductances of the left and right halves are obtained as follows: \begin{align*} {L_{\text {left}}} & = \frac {L_{\text {off} }}{2} - {k_{1}}\sqrt {{L_{\text {off} }} \cdot \frac {L_{\text {off} }}{2}} \\ & \quad + {k_{2}}\sqrt {{L_{\text {off} }} \cdot \frac {L_{\text {off} }}{2}} - \frac {1}{2}{M_{\text {opp},\text {off} }} \tag {21}\\ {L_{\text {right}}} & =\frac {L_{\text {off} }}{2} - {k_{2}}\sqrt {{L_{\text {off} }} \cdot \frac {L_{\text {off} }}{2}} \\ & \quad + {k_{1}}\sqrt {{L_{\text {off} }} \cdot \frac {L_{\text {off} }}{2}} - \frac {1}{2}{M_{\text {opp},\text {off} }}. \tag {22}\end{align*}
Fig. 11.
Circuit equivalence for the mode-3 transformer with the switched inductor off.

app.p:
For simplicity, we assume that $|I_{{Gm},1}|{\,}={\,}|I_{{Gm},2}|{\,}={\,}|I_{{Gm},3}|{\,}={\,}|I_{{Gm},4}|{\,}={\,}1$, which accordingly implies that $A_{0}=R_{T}$. Moreover, $I_{{Gm},i}$ is in phase with $V_{T,i}$. Substituting the parameters ($I_{{Gm},2}$, $I_{B,4}$, $I_{B,1}$, and $I_{T,2}$) into (34), we obtain
\begin{equation*} {e^{j{\theta _{2}}}} + \frac {\alpha }{r}\left ({{2 - 2{e^{j{\theta _{2}}}}}}\right ) = \alpha \left ({{e^{j{\theta _{2}}}}}\right )\left ({{1 + j2{K_{2}}}}\right ) \tag {37}\end{equation*}where $r=R_{\text {on} }/R_{T}$. Equating the real and imaginary parts on both sides of (37), we obtain
\begin{align*} \frac {2\alpha }{r}\left ({{1 - \cos {\theta _{2}}}}\right ) + \cos {\theta _{2}} & = \alpha \cos {\theta _{2}} - 2\alpha {K_{2}}\sin {\theta _{2}} \tag {38}\\ \left ({{1 - \frac {2\alpha }{r}}}\right )\sin {\theta _{2}} & = \alpha \sin {\theta _{2}} + 2\alpha {K_{2}}\cos {\theta _{2}}. \tag {39}\end{align*}
